<package xmlns="http://www.idpf.org/2007/opf" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" version="3.0" unique-identifier="pub-identifier" prefix="ibooks: http://vocabulary.itunes.apple.com/rdf/ibooks/vocabulary-extensions-1.0/"><metadata><dc:identifier id="pub-identifier">9781492075455</dc:identifier><meta id="meta-identifier" property="dcterms:identifier">9781492075455</meta><dc:title id="pub-title">Microservices: Up and Running</dc:title><meta property="dcterms:title" id="meta-title">Microservices: Up and Running</meta><dc:language id="pub-language">en</dc:language><meta property="dcterms:language" id="meta-language">en</meta><meta property="dcterms:modified">2020-11-25T16:48:30Z</meta><dc:publisher>O'Reilly Media, Inc.</dc:publisher><meta property="dcterms:publisher">O'Reilly Media, Inc.</meta><dc:date>2020-11-25</dc:date><meta property="dcterms:date">2020-11-25</meta><dc:description>This book solves the following problems for users: What does a good microservices project look like? Are the decisions we are making for our project the right ones? How do we come up with a good MS design that fits our own context as quickly as possible? Where should I spend time thinking/designing and where should I just implement best practices?</dc:description><meta property="dcterms:description">This book solves the following problems for users: What does a good microservices project look like? Are the decisions we are making for our project the right ones? How do we come up with a good MS design that fits our own context as quickly as possible? Where should I spend time thinking/designing and where should I just implement best practices?</meta><dc:creator>Ronnie Mitra and Irakli Nadareishvili</dc:creator><meta property="dcterms:creator">Ronnie Mitra and Irakli Nadareishvili</meta><meta name="cover" content="cover-image"/><meta property="ibooks:specified-fonts">true</meta></metadata><manifest><item id="toc.ncx" href="toc.ncx" media-type=